n$$\ny={e}^{4x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x),\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-8{y}^{′}+65y=0,\\,y(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064694/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ca21b24c36ae88f7c5babf314148d41fcf25ca864e6228b45ac387d5e81d0c4"} {"question" "q-130370" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions. \n\n- Substituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nP=2.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=1,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4{e}^{4x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x)+{e}^{4x}(-7P\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x+7Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x).\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=1$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n4P+7Q=1.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $P=2$ gives $Q=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{4x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064694/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ca21b24c36ae88f7c5babf314148d41fcf25ca864e6228b45ac387d5e81d0c4"} {"question" "q-130370" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064694/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2c4e29dd259a0b73c75efb40cbcc43d96a86a409fde5103019e0c01c90e2bf14"} {"question" "q-130370" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064694/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ca21b24c36ae88f7c5babf314148d41fcf25ca864e6228b45ac387d5e8