operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+{e}^{-3x}(-2P\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x).\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=-4$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n-3P+2Q=-4.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $P=2$ gives $Q=1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064694/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ca21b24c36ae88f7c5babf314148d41fcf25ca864e6228b45ac387d5e81d0c4"} {"question" "q-94030" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064694/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2c4e29dd259a0b73c75efb40cbcc43d96a86a409fde5103019e0c01c90e2bf14"} {"question" "q-94030" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064694/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ca21b24c36ae88f7c5babf314148d41fcf25ca864e6228b45ac387d5e81d0c4"} {"question" "q-94030" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y={e}^{-3x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)"] ["math" "y={e}^{-3x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)"] ["math" "y={e}^{-3x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)"] ["math" "y={e}^{-3x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)"] ["math" "y={e}^{-3x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064694/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ca21b24c36ae88f7c5babf314148d41fcf25ca864e6228b45ac387d5e81d0c4"} {"question" "q-94030" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y={e}^{-3x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064694/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ca21b24c36ae88f7c5babf314148d41fcf25ca864e6228b45ac387d5e81d0c4"} {"question" "q-94025" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+5y=0,\\,y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={e}^{-\\pi },\\,{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=6{e}^{-\\pi }.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064694/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ca21b24c36ae88f7c5babf314148d41fcf25ca864e6228b45ac387d5e81d0c4"} {"question" "q-94025" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +5 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4)-4+5 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda +2 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{-2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={e}^{-\\pi }$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q{e}^{-\\pi } & ={e}^{-\\pi } \\\\ Q & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=6{e}^{-\\pi },$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2{e}^{-2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+{e}^{-2x}(-P\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=6{e}^{-\\pi }$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2Q{e}^{-\\pi }-P{e}^{-\\pi } & =6{e}^{-\\pi } \\\\ -2Q-P & =6 \\\\ -2-P & =6 \\\\ P & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=-8$ and $Q=1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{-2x}(-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064694/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ca21b24c36ae88f7c5babf314148d41fcf25ca864e6228b45ac387d5e81d0c4"} {"question" "q-94025" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064694/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2c4e29dd259a0b73c75efb40cbcc43d96a86a409fde5103019e0c01c90e2bf14"} {"question" "q-94025" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064694/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ca21b24c36ae88f7c5babf314148d41fcf25ca864e6228b45ac387d5e81d0c4"} {"question" "q-94025" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-2{e}^{-2x}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)"] ["math" "y=-{e}^{-2x}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)"] ["math" "y={e}^{-2x}(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)"] ["math" "y={e}^{-2x}(-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)"] ["math" "y={e}^{-2x}(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064694/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ca21b24c36ae88f7c5babf314148d41fcf25ca864e6228b45ac387d5e81d0c4"} {"question" "q-94025" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y={e}^{-2x}(-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064694/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ca21b24c36ae88f7c5babf314148d41fcf25ca864e6228b45ac387d5e81d0c4"} {"question" "e-6861" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + y - 2y =0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{x} + Be^{- 2 x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' + y - 2y =0,\n\\ \\ y(1) = e, \\ \\ y'(1) = e ^{-2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064694/example-6861.json" "file_sha256" "29a48c4e7a108513f1bd612f4c922bedf316361db0a8f091fa683cfb96545cb2"} {"question" "e-6861" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(1)=e$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e & =A{e}^{1}+B{e}^{-2(1)} \\\\ e & =Ae+B{e}^{-2} \\\\ A & =1-B{e}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y'(1) = e^{-2},$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\ny' = Ae^x - 2Be^{-x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y'(1)=e^{-2}$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2} & =A{e}^{1}-2B{e}^{-2(1)} \\\\ {e}^{-2} & =Ae-2B{e}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A=1-B{e}^{-3} \\\\ {e}^{-2}=Ae-2B{e}^{-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A = 1 - B e ^{-3}$ in the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2} & =(1-B{e}^{-3})e-2B{e}^{-2} \\\\ 1 & ={e}^{3}-B-2B \\\\ B & =\\frac{{e}^{3}-1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting $B= \\dfrac {e ^ 3 - 1} 3$ back into the equation $A = 1-Be^{-3}$ gives\n\n$$\nA = 1 -  \\dfrac {(e ^ 3 - 1) e ^ {-3}} 3 =   \\dfrac {2 +  e ^ {-3}} 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution of the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny =  \\dfrac {  e ^ {-3} + 2} 3  e^{x} + \\dfrac {e ^ 3 - 1} 3 e^{-2x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064694/example-6861.json" "file_sha256" "29a48c4e7a108513f1bd612f4c922bedf316361db0a8f091fa683cfb96545cb2"} {"question" "e-6862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' - 4 y' + 4y =0,\n\\qquad y(0) =  3, \\qquad y'(0) = 2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064694/example-6862.json" "file_sha256" "c6d52ba7ac756a8acf293033fa3db3b11e2fcd647992ca4c17c49ddc6cfffda2"} {"question" "e-6862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have a repeated root $\\lambda = 2,$ so we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\ny = A e ^ { 2 x} + B x e ^ {2 x}\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=3$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =A{e}^{2(0)}+B(0){e}^{2(0)} \\\\ 3 & =A{e}^{0}+0 \\\\ 3 & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y' (0) = 2,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\ny'  = 2 A e ^ {2 x} +  B e ^ { 2 x} + 2 B x e ^{ 2 x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y'(0)=2$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2A{e}^{2(0)}+B{e}^{2(0)}+2B(0){e}^{2(0)} \\\\ 2 & =2A{e}^{0}+B{e}^{0}+0 \\\\ 2 & =2A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3=A \\\\ 2=2A+B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=3$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =6+B \\\\ B & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=3$ and $B=-4,$ and therefore the solution is \n\n$$\ny = 3 e ^ { 2 x } - 4 x e ^ { 2 x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064694/example-6862.json" "file_sha256" "c6d52ba7ac756a8acf293033fa3db3b11e2fcd647992ca4c17c49ddc6cfffda2"} {"question" "e-6791" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny'' + y = 0, \\quad y(\\pi) = 2, \\quad y'(\\pi) = -1\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064694/example-6791.json" "file_sha256" "4b3b4eedd309399cd79486821d90accca0e220011dbf8d19d7f8e3f78ffb25df"} {"question" "e-6791" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\ny = A \\cos x + B \\sin x\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find constants $A$ and $B.$ To do this, we apply the initial conditions $y(\\pi)=2,$ $y'(\\pi)=-1.$\n\n- Substituting $y(\\pi) = 2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\pi ) & =2 \\\\ A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi  & =2 \\\\ -A & =2 \\\\ A & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Computing $y'(x),$ we get\n\n$$\ny' =  - A \\sin{x} + B\\cos{x},\n$$\n\nand substituting $y'(\\pi)=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\pi ) & =-1 \\\\ -A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  & =-1 \\\\ -B & =-1 \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = -2\\cos{x} + \\sin{x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064694/example-6791.json" "file_sha256" "4b3b4eedd309399cd79486821d90accca0e220011dbf8d19d7f8e3f78ffb25df"} {"question" "e-7051" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' - 6y' + 25y=0, \\quad y(0)= 2,  \\quad y'(0) = 5, \\quad t\\gt 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064694/example-7051.json" "file_sha256" "b964c0b61485530e0a8c3a18c713ff0330db892220bebf4d9a96246d5bb91632"} {"question" "e-7051" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +9)-9+25 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-3)^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda -3 & =4\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =3\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\ny = e^{3x} \\left( P \\cos 4x + Q \\sin 4x \\right)\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0) = 2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n2 = P.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y'(0) = 5,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3{e}^{3x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)+{e}^{3x}(-4P\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+4Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y'(0) = 5$ and $P=2$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =3{e}^{0}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡0)+{e}^{0}(-4(2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+4Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\ 5 & =6+4Q \\\\ -1 & =4Q \\\\ Q & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = e^{3x} \\left( 2 \\cos 4x - \\dfrac{1}{4} \\sin 4x \\right).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064694/example-7051.json" "file_sha256" "b964c0b61485530e0a8c3a18c713ff0330db892220bebf4d9a96246d5bb91632"}] "usage" {"e-6791" [] "q-7788" [] "e-7051" [] "e-6861" [] "e-6862" []} "basis" 2119 "retractions" [[17592186152698 :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n$y^{″} + 7y^{′} + 12y = 0$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$y = Ae^{-3x} + Be^{-4x}$,\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$y^{″} + 7y^{′} + 12y = 0, y(0) =-3, y^{′}(0) = 2$."] [17592186151921 :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$y'' + y - 2y =0$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$y = Ae^{x} + Be^{-2 x}$,\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n$y'' + y - 2y =0, \\ \\ y(1) = e, \\ \\ y'(1) = e ^{-2}$."] [17592186151921 :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(1)=e$ into our general solution gives\n$e|= Ae^{1} + Be^{-2(1)}; e|= Ae + Be^{-2}; A|= 1 - Be^{-3}$.\n- To apply the condition $y'(1) = e^{-2}$, we first differentiate $y$ to get\n$y' = Ae^x - 2Be^{-x}$.\nThen, we substitute $y'(1)=e^{-2}$ into the above to get\n$e^{-2}|= Ae^{1} - 2Be^{-2(1)}; e^{-2}|= Ae - 2Be^{-2}$.\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{A = 1 - Be^{-3}; e^{-2} = Ae - 2Be^{-2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nA = 1 - B e ^{-3}\n$$\n\nin the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{-2} &= (1 - Be^{-3})e - 2Be^{-2} \\\\\n1 &= e^{3} - B - 2B \\\\\nB &= \\frac{e^{3} - 1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting\n\n$$\nB= \\dfrac {e ^ 3 - 1} 3\n$$\n\nback into the equation\n\n$$\nA = 1-Be^{-3}\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\nA = 1 - \\dfrac {(e ^ 3 - 1) e ^ {-3}} 3 = \\dfrac {2 + e ^ {-3}} 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution of the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac {e ^ {-3} + 2} 3 e^{x} + \\dfrac {e ^ 3 - 1} 3 e^{-2x}\n$$"] [17592186151922 :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n$y'' - 4 y' + 4y =0, \\qquad y(0) = 3, \\qquad y'(0) = 2$."] [17592186151922 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} - 4λ + 4 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 2)^{2} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have a repeated root\n\n$$\n\\lambda = 2\n$$\n\nso we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\ny = A e ^ {2 x} + B x e ^ {2 x}\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=3$ into our general solution gives\n$3|= Ae^{2(0)} + B(0)e^{2(0)}; 3|= Ae^{0} + 0; 3|= A$.\n- To apply the condition $y' (0) = 2$, we first differentiate $y$ to get\n$y' = 2 A e ^ {2 x} + B e ^ {2 x} + 2 B x e ^{2 x}$.\nThen, we substitute $y'(0)=2$ into the above to get\n$2|= 2Ae^{2(0)} + Be^{2(0)} + 2B(0)e^{2(0)}; 2|= 2Ae^{0} + Be^{0} + 0; 2|= 2A + B$.\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{3 = A; 2 = 2A + B\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=3$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 &= 6 + B \\\\\nB &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=3$ and $B=-4$, and therefore the solution is\n\n$$\ny = 3 e ^ {2 x} - 4 x e ^ {2 x}\n$$"] [17592186151910 :question/problem "Solve the following initial value problem:\n\n$y'' + y = 0, \\quad y(\\pi) = 2, \\quad y'(\\pi) = -1$"] [17592186151910 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} + 1 &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} &= -1 \\\\\nλ &= ± \\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\ny = A \\cos x + B \\sin x\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find constants $A$ and $B$. To do this, we apply the initial conditions $y(\\pi)=2$, $y'(\\pi)=-1$.\n\n- Substituting $y(\\pi) = 2$ into the general solution gives\n$y(π)|= 2; A\\cos π + B\\sin π|= 2; - A|= 2; A|=-2$.\n- Computing $y'(x)$, we get\n$y' =-A \\sin{x} + B\\cos{x}$,\nand substituting $y'(\\pi)=-1$, we get\n$y^{′}(π)|=-1; - A\\sin π + B\\cos π|=-1; - B|=-1; B|= 1$.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = -2\\cos{x} + \\sin{x}\n$$"] [17592186151935 :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$y'' - 6y' + 25y=0, \\quad y(0)= 2, \\quad y'(0) = 5, \\quad t\\gt 0$."] [17592186151935 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} - 6λ + 25 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ^{2} - 6λ + 9) - 9 + 25 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ^{2} - 3)^{2} + 16 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 3)^{2} &= -16 \\\\\nλ - 3 &= 4\\textrm{i} \\\\\nλ &= 3 ± 4\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\ny = e^{3x} \\left(P \\cos 4x + Q \\sin 4x \\right)\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0) = 2$ into the general solution gives\n$2 = P$.\n- To apply the condition $y'(0) = 5$, we first differentiate $y$ to get\n$y^{′}|= 3e^{3x}(P\\cos 4x + Q\\sin 4x) + e^{3x}(-4P\\sin 4x + 4Q\\cos 4x)$\nThen, we substitute $y'(0) = 5$ and $P=2$ into the above to get\n$5|= 3e^{0}(2\\cos 0 + Q\\sin 0) + e^{0}(-4(2)\\sin 0 + 4Q\\cos 0); 5|= 6 + 4Q; - 1|= 4Q; Q|=-\\frac{1}{4}$.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = e^{3x} \\left(2 \\cos 4x - \\dfrac{1}{4} \\sin 4x \\right)\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186151099 17592186151100 17592186151101 17592186151102 17592186151103] "immutable_fields" [[17592186151098 :answer-field/correct] [17592186151098 :answer-field/id] [17592186151098 :answer-field/key] [17592186151098 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
